<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  application/vnd.taarib.ruqaa — the shared-mime-info declaration.

  Installed to /usr/share/mime/packages/ by bundle.linux.deb.files, where the
  shared-mime-info package's dpkg trigger picks it up and runs
  update-mime-database. Until that runs, a .ruqaa is "unknown data" to every
  file manager on the system: the desktop entry's MimeType= line says which
  application opens a type, and this file says what the type IS.

  bundle.linux.appimage.files puts the same declaration at the same path inside
  the AppDir. Nothing on the host reads it from a mounted image, so that copy
  buys nothing on its own — it is there so that an integrated AppImage, and any
  future format built from the same AppDir, carries a complete declaration
  rather than a dangling MimeType= line. docs/tawzee/linux.md §3a is the
  full picture.

  The media type string is the one canonical value, declared in Rust as
  taarib_ruqaa::NAW_MUHTAWA. It is spelled here, in tauri.conf.json's
  fileAssociations, and in taarib.desktop's MimeType= — three copies of one
  string, and any drift between them registers a type nothing opens.

  DETECTION — magic before glob.

  A glob only knows what a file is named. The container opens with a four-byte
  magic (taarib_ruqaa::SIHR = "TRQ1", crates/taarib-ruqaa/src/tarwisa.rs), so
  content detection is available and is strictly better: a patch renamed by a
  browser download, a patch arriving over a pipe, and a .ruqaa that is really
  a zip all resolve correctly.

  The magic deliberately does NOT also match format_version (u16 LE at offset
  4, currently 1). The version varies within one container format; the type
  does not. Pinning it here would silently stop detecting a version 2 patch by
  content and fall back to the extension, which is the failure this rule
  exists to avoid. The generation digit already lives inside the magic itself.

  Priority 70 rather than the default 50: four fixed bytes at offset zero is a
  specific match, and it should win over anything sniffing the same prefix.

  ICONS.

  <icon> names the exact icon a file manager draws for this type; the name is
  the media type with '/' and '+' replaced by '-', which is the freedesktop
  convention, so the file installs as
  hicolor/<size>/mimetypes/application-vnd.taarib.ruqaa.png. <generic-icon>
  is the fallback for a theme that has no such file — package-x-generic, one
  of the enumerated names the DTD permits, because a patch is a sealed
  container rather than a text document.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/vnd.taarib.ruqaa">
    <comment>Taarib translation patch</comment>
    <comment xml:lang="ar">رقعة تعريب</comment>
    <icon name="application-vnd.taarib.ruqaa"/>
    <generic-icon name="package-x-generic"/>
    <magic priority="70">
      <match type="string" value="TRQ1" offset="0"/>
    </magic>
    <glob pattern="*.ruqaa"/>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
